<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <parent>
        <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy-client-java</artifactId>
        <groupId>bz.stub.parallelconsumer</groupId>
        <version>0.6.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    </parent>
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <name>Parallel Consumer - Proxy Client Java Harness Lane</name>
    <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy-client-java-harness</artifactId>

    <!--
        THE ONE PLACE ON THE JVM SIDE THAT DEPENDS ON THE SIDECAR, AND THE REASON IT IS A MODULE
        RATHER THAN A SCOPE (astubbs#242).

        THE HAZARD IT REMOVES. A test-scope dependency is not transitive on the CLASSPATH, but it
        IS an edge in the Maven REACTOR: `-pl <anything that depends on java-grpc> -am` walks the
        declared dependencies of every module it reaches, test scope included. If java-grpc
        test-depended on parallel-consumer-proxy, any client wrapping it - the Kotlin client, Scala
        next - would drag the sidecar into an ordinary build, and `bin/build.sh` opens with `clean`,
        so the routine build of a wrapper would delete the sidecar jar other languages' tests spawn.
        The measurement that says the hazard is absent is a reactor list, not an assertion:

            ./mvnw -pl :parallel-consumer-proxy-client-kotlin -am validate

        must not print parallel-consumer-proxy. Rerun it before adding any dependency here or in a
        client module.

        WHY NOT A PROFILE, which is how the non-JVM clients answer the same question. Their harness
        lane is opt-in because their tests need a foreign toolchain anyway. Here the test needs
        nothing but the JVM already running the build, and it is the Java client's only evidence
        against a real server; a profile would take it out of the default lane, and a test that does
        not run is not a passing test.

        WHAT IS IN IT TODAY, and it is deliberately less than the name promises. The sidecar on this
        branch hosts no Parallel Consumer engine - it binds, admits a connection and refuses every
        session with UNIMPLEMENTED - so the only thing that can be proved end to end is the
        handshake: that the gRPC transport's own connect path reaches a real server's real service
        and reports what it was told.

        THE ENGINE-BACKED CONFORMANCE RUN IS NOT HERE, AND IT IS NOT THE CONFORMANCE RUNG'S EITHER.
        This comment used to say it arrived with the rung stacked above; that rung has landed the
        suite, the scenarios, the core control arm and the java-direct cell, and could not land this
        one - the gRPC cell needs an engine to dispatch, and writing a stand-in for it would make
        every binding's agreement a statement about a stub. So it waits on the engine, and
        TheEngineArrivingMustBringTheGrpcCellTest in this module's test tree fails the build if the
        engine arrives without it. The compile-scope harness classpath a JVM client declares to spawn
        a sidecar as a child process waits on the same rung.

        This module publishes nothing (the clients aggregator skips deploy and install for the whole
        tree) and has no main sources: it is a classpath and a test tree, deliberately.
    -->

    <properties>
        <!-- the sidecar and the gRPC transport both require it; see java-grpc's pom for the reasoning -->
        <release.target>17</release.target>
    </properties>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <!--
                The parent's SpotBugs gate is inherited and NOT switched off, even though this module
                has no main sources: the root pom sets includeTests=true, so the analysis has this
                module's test tree to read and its one finding is declined below with its reason. An
                opt-out would also silently exempt the first main source anyone adds here.

                What is configured is only the filter's location. ${project.basedir} rather than a
                shared path in the parent: a relative excludeFilterFile resolves against the module
                being built, so a filter declared once in the parent would be looked for inside each
                child anyway. ${project.basedir} rather than a shared path in the parent: a relative
                excludeFilterFile resolves against the module being built, so a filter declared once
                in the parent would be looked for inside each child anyway.
            -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>com.github.spotbugs</groupId>
                <artifactId>spotbugs-maven-plugin</artifactId>
                <configuration>
                    <excludeFilterFile>${project.basedir}/spotbugs-exclude.xml</excludeFilterFile>
                </configuration>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>

    <dependencies>
        <!--
            THE SIDECAR, at compile scope. Compile rather than test so it flows transitively: a JVM
            client that needs one declares this ONE module in its own opt-in profile and gets the
            set, instead of restating dependencies and drifting from them. Nothing consumes it that
            way yet; the scope is chosen for what the module is for, and the alternative would have
            to be changed back the moment the first wrapper arrives.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>bz.stub.parallelconsumer</groupId>
            <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy</artifactId>
            <version>${project.version}</version>
        </dependency>

        <!-- the transport under test -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>bz.stub.parallelconsumer</groupId>
            <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy-client-java-grpc</artifactId>
            <version>${project.version}</version>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <!--
            TestConventionRules, the shared ArchUnit rule library. Core's test-jar is the only
            engine-side artifact this module names directly, and it is not the hazardous one: core
            is already in the reactor of anything that reaches the protocol module.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>bz.stub.parallelconsumer</groupId>
            <artifactId>parallel-consumer-core</artifactId>
            <version>${project.version}</version>
            <classifier>tests</classifier>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <!--
            Declared explicitly rather than leaned on transitively: core's test-jar reaches for
            commons-lang3, and test scope is not transitive, so relying on core to supply it here
            would break the moment core stopped using it.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.apache.commons</groupId>
            <artifactId>commons-lang3</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>

</project>
